body:
`Index` describes one file. `Index::write` stamps that file's length and mtime, and the free function `is_current` re-stats one data path against one index path. So `Aggregate<IndexedFasta>` carries one `Index` per part, and the only thing the aggregate adds is `starts`, one `usize` per part. Nothing on disk records that the parts belong together. A record's global index depends on the order the parts were passed in, so that order exists only in the call that built the aggregate.

## Proposal

A manifest: an ordered list of data files, each with its stamp and record count, in a file of its own. It points at each file's own `.saidx` (sail-9d931d) and holds no offsets. A part's `.saidx` stays usable without the manifest.

```rust
// libsail::manifest
impl Manifest {
    /// Index every path that lacks a current `.saidx`, and list them all.
    pub fn build(paths: impl IntoIterator<Item = impl AsRef<Path>>, format: Format) -> Result<Manifest>;
    pub fn read(path: &Path) -> Result<Manifest>;
    pub fn write(&self, path: &Path) -> Result<()>;

    pub fn format(&self) -> Format;
    /// The data files, in the order that fixes the global index.
    pub fn paths(&self) -> &[PathBuf];
    /// Each part's first global position, with the total on the end.
    pub fn starts(&self) -> &[usize];
    /// The parts whose data file no longer matches its stamp, by position.
    pub fn stale(&self) -> Vec<usize>;
}
```

- `build` writes a named `.saidx` beside each path whose index is missing or stale, the same file `sail index` writes. It reuses a current one and reads the count from its header.
- The manifest stamps each data file itself. `stale()` stats N files and opens no `.saidx`, and `starts()` comes from the stored counts. A part's `.saidx` is opened only when a record is read from it.
- A part whose data file cannot be stat'd is stale, so a caller can name it (sail-978552).
- Paths are written relative to the manifest's directory, with `/` between components, so a directory of parts and its manifest can move together. `read` resolves them against the manifest's directory. A path with no relative route, such as one on another Windows drive, or one that is not UTF-8, is a write error.
- The extension is `.samanifest`, with magic `SLMF` and a version byte of its own.

## Layout

| region | size |
|---|---|
| magic `SLMF` | 4 |
| version | 1 |
| format | 1 |
| part count `p` | 8 |
| per part: path length `L` | 4 |
| per part: path, UTF-8 | `L` |
| per part: data length, mtime seconds, mtime nanoseconds | 8 + 8 + 4 |
| per part: record count | 8 |

## Out of scope

`sail index` over several inputs is sail-2413e6. Reading records through a manifest, as an `Aggregate` or a `Readers`, comes after it.

## Cost

Additive: a new public module. The `.saidx` layout does not change.
